## Description
Today is Alice's first day at the Wayne Enterprises' Security Operations Center. Lucius sits Alice down and gives her first assignment: A memo from Gotham City Police Department (GCPD). 
Apparently GCPD has found evidence online (http://pastebin.com/Gw6dWjS9) that the website www.imreallynotbatman.com hosted on Wayne Enterprises' IP address space has been compromised. 
The group has multiple objectives... but a key aspect of their modus operandi is to deface websites in order to embarrass their victim. 
Lucius has asked Alice to determine if www.imreallynotbatman.com. (the personal blog of Wayne Corporations CEO) was really compromised.

Question 1: What is the likely IPv4 address of someone from the Po1s0n1vy group scanning imreallynotbatman.com for web application vulnerabilities?
Solution: 
1. In Splunk I specified index="botsv1" sourcetype="stream:http" imreallynotbatman.com
2. looked at Source IP field and entered in 40.80.148.42 since it had the highest count.

Writeup: Still learning how to use splunk the fields are very convenient. How hard can it get!

Question 2: What company created the web vulnerability scanner used by Po1s0n1vy? Type the company name.
Answer guidance: For example "Microsoft" or "Oracle"
Solution:
1. simply searched up web vulnerabilty scanner and saw Acunetix Web Vulnerability Scanner to which I entered Acunetix and got it right

Question 3: What content management system is imreallynotbatman.com likely using?
Answer guidance: Please do not include punctuation such as . , ! ? in your answer. We are looking for alpha characters only.
Solution:
Quite literally the same as Question 2

Question 4: What is the name of the file that defaced the imreallynotbatman.com website? Please submit only the name of the file with extension?
Answer guidance: For example "notepad.exe" or "favicon.ico"
Solution: 
1. Find the webserver through destination ip when filtering imreallynotbatman.com since that is where we send requests to.
2. We then use the destination ip we just found of 192.168.250.70 as the source ip and remove the imreallynotbatman.com filter and specify GET to see which file was downloaded. 
3. We see that poisonivy-is-coming-for-you-batman.jpeg is the file downloaded through a quick search which lets us complete the level.

Writeup: This was really hard for me. I needed a hint and the destination ip and source ip switching weren't obvious nor intuitive to me. But still fun to tinker with searching.
Needed sometime to understand the meaning of deface and filter what I was looking for.
